    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Clerks can now only settle claims whose due is at or below the sheet's clerk limit, and stewards can settle anything. All 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Settling:** a new check runs after the settle works out the claim's due and before anything is saved. A clerk settling a claim above the limit is refused with a 409, and the reason shows in the claim's `claim-error`. Nothing is stored or written to the audit file. A due of n/a passes any limit, and so does every claim when the limit is blank. The rule that the opener can't settle their own claim still applies.\n- **Roles:** stored per sheet and user name, either `clerk` or `steward`. A name with no stored role counts as a clerk. Anything else, like `boss`, is refused with a 400 and writes nothing. Saving a role writes `ROLE_SET \u2026 user=<name> role=<role>`.\n- **Clerk limit:** stored per sheet. It must be blank or an amount with up to two decimals, and it is kept as two decimals. Saving it writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=clerk-limit value=<limit>` every time, even when the value hasn't changed, because the third test expects that.\n- **Storage:** a new migration, `V17__setting_role.sql`, adds a settings table and a roles table.\n- **Screen:** a new Settings section under Periods (`features/settings/Settings.tsx`). It has the clerk-limit field with its current value, and a role form with a table of assigned roles.\n\n**Things to know**\n- The settings save sends only fields you've edited. To clear the limit, type something into the field and delete it, then save; pressing save without touching the field does nothing.\n- Settings use one endpoint per key (`PUT /api/sheets/{sheetId}/settings/{key}`), so future settings can reuse it. Roles are set with `PUT /api/sheets/{sheetId}/roles/{userName}`.",
